<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Dev override (docs/architecture/repositories.md): set the AgoCalendarDevOverride environment
    variable to swap Ago.Platform.* PackageReferences for ProjectReferences into a sibling
    ../ago-platform checkout, for a change that spans this repository and the platform.

      AgoCalendarDevOverride=true dotnet build            (bash)
      $env:AgoCalendarDevOverride = 'true'; dotnet build  (PowerShell)

    Read it as "the override *this repository* honours", not "the override that points at
    ago-calendar" - it retargets the same Ago.Platform.* references ago-chat's own
    AgoPlatformDevOverride retargets there. The two are deliberately different names rather than one
    shared one (`20-00`): a single variable would silently retarget *both* products' builds in the
    same shell, so switching ago-calendar onto unpublished platform source would also switch
    ago-chat onto it - and a green ago-chat build against source that is not published yet is
    exactly the API break the package boundary exists to catch.

    Nothing in this repository changes when the override is used, so a merged branch cannot
    accidentally keep it switched on - CI never sets the variable and always builds against the
    published package.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AgoCalendarDevOverride Condition="'$(AgoCalendarDevOverride)' == ''">false</AgoCalendarDevOverride>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
